    # Japanese. Machine translated, NOT reviewed by a native speaker - see the header of
    # Tlc.Localization.psm1. Safe for what it covers: field labels, buttons and hints.
    # Nothing that reaches a bundle is translated.
    #
    # Product names stay in English on purpose. "Teams admin center", "Teams Rooms" and
    # "PowerShell" are what the controls in those products actually say, so translating them
    # would send an engineer looking for something that does not exist under that name.
    #
    # One word per concept: katakana "ログ" for log and "トレース" for trace, everywhere.
    #
    # Sentences end with the ideographic full stop U+3002, not an ASCII period. The
    # punctuation-parity test in Tlc.Localization.Tests.ps1 knows that, for the same reason
    # it knows Hindi ends a sentence with a danda: demanding an ASCII period here would be
    # demanding the wrong punctuation and calling it a standard.
    #
    # THIS FILE MUST BE SAVED AS UTF-8 WITH A BOM. Without one, Import-PowerShellDataFile on
    # Windows PowerShell 5.1 reads the bytes as the ANSI codepage and the whole table
    # becomes mojibake, while PowerShell 7 reads it correctly either way.

